course: dictionary
lesson: 3232
title: Entitlement: a right, or a feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An entitlement is something you have a right to have or do. Context helps you spot which meaning is being used. «Mum checked Ben's holiday entitlement: 28 days a year.» Here it means the time off that Ben is allowed by his work, so it is about a right.
explain: The second meaning is about feelings. «Ruby thought she should always get the last biscuit, and she was cross when she didn't.» Ruby is acting as if she deserves special treatment. That is what a sense of entitlement means, and writers use it to show that a character is not being fair.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping in a simple word. In the first meaning, «right» fits: «Ben's holiday right is 28 days.» In the second meaning, «right» can make the sentence odd, so look at the clues around the word, such as whether someone is being unfair or greedy.
fact: An entitlement is something you have a right to have or do.
fact: Entitlement can also mean a feeling that you deserve special treatment when you do not; this use is disapproving.
fact: Common phrases are «holiday entitlement», «sense of entitlement» and «entitlement to».
fact: In maths, an entitlement can be a number of days or an amount of money that you need to work out.
fact: «Entitlement reform» is a phrase grown-ups use in America. It means changing the rules to cut pensions and welfare payments.
exercise: «Mum checked her holiday entitlement before booking the trip.» What does entitlement mean here? | A map showing the way to the beach | A sort of holiday suitcase | *The time off you have a right to | A trip that has been cancelled
exercise: Her teacher says Priya has a sense of entitlement after she demands the best seat. What does that suggest? | She is feeling very tired after a long day | *She thinks she deserves special treatment, even when she does not | She has been given a right to vote in class | She is sure she will win the race
exercise: A worker's holiday entitlement is 28 days. Sam has already taken 12 days. How many days of entitlement are left? | 14 | 40 | 28 | *16
test: «Every worker has an entitlement to a break after six hours.» What is another word for entitlement, as it is used here? | *right | noise | colour | danger
test: Mum says, «Ben has an entitlement to free school meals.» What does this mean? | Ben must eat his lunch in the library | Ben does not like school dinners | *Ben has a right to free school meals | Ben has been sent home early
exam: Gran says, «Stop showing a sense of entitlement and wait your turn.» What does Gran mean? | Gran is giving you an entitlement to a prize | Gran wants you to shout louder to get noticed | Gran thinks your holiday entitlement is too small | *Gran wants you to be patient and not expect special treatment
